NotebookLoader.register({"id":"data-analysis-python","title":"Data analysis with Python","category":"Data & AI","status":"ready","description":"Investigate fictional support tickets with NumPy, pandas and charts. Validate the data, explain comparisons and produce a reproducible report.","level":"Foundations to reproducible analysis","prerequisites":["Python functions, lists, dictionaries and basic tests.","Basic averages and percentages; study practical maths and statistics alongside this path."],"outcomes":["Validate a tabular data contract.","Calculate summaries with explicit denominators.","Join data and create labeled charts.","Deliver a tested report with clear limits."],"setup":["Download the practice kit and read README.md.","Run the standard-library dataset checks with Python 3.14 or newer.","For executable analysis, use Python 3.14+ and install requirements.txt in a virtual environment. The tested interpreter is Python 3.14."],"nextSteps":["Study machine learning foundations after validating the reporting pipeline."],"sources":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html"},{"title":"NumPy beginner guide","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/absolute_beginners.html"},{"title":"Matplotlib quick start","url":"https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/explain/quick_start.html"}],"lessons":[{"id":"question-and-grain","title":"1. Ask a question and define one row","takeaway":"Define the population and what one row represents before calculating a metric.","stage":"foundation","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Our fictional support desk asks what share of completed tickets took more than 24 hours to resolve. The supplied file contains one row per completed ticket, not one row per message. A message table would give busy tickets more weight if you averaged its rows directly. Write the unit of observation beside the question.","The file covers 24 fictional tickets created on 24 consecutive dates. It is a teaching sample without open tickets. Its result describes those rows only. A service-wide claim would also need a collection process, a reporting period and a policy for unresolved tickets. Define the denominator before choosing pandas operations."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Two tickets take 10 and 30 hours; one has five messages and the other has one. At one row per ticket the mean is 20 hours. Repeating each ticket once per message gives six rows totalling 80 hours, a mean of 80/6 hours. That weights busy tickets more heavily and answers a different question."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ntickets = pd.DataFrame({\"ticket_id\": [\"T1\", \"T2\"],\n                        \"hours\": [10, 30],\n                        \"messages\": [5, 1]})\nmessage_rows = tickets.loc[tickets.index.repeat(tickets[\"messages\"])]\n\nprint(tickets[\"hours\"].mean())\nprint(len(message_rows), message_rows[\"hours\"].sum())\nprint(round(message_rows[\"hours\"].mean(), 2))\n\n# Output:\n# 20.0\n# 6 80\n# 13.33"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Two completed tickets take 8 and 40 hours. What fraction breached the greater-than-24-hour rule?","solution":"One of two tickets breached, so the fraction is 0.5 or 50%.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Count completed tickets, not messages.","Apply the strict 24-hour rule to each ticket before dividing."]},"quiz":{"question":"A file contains 6 completed tickets and 18 messages. Two tickets took more than 24 hours. What is the ticket breach rate?","options":["2/6, about 33.3%.","2/18, about 11.1%.","6/18, about 33.3%."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Two of the six tickets breached. Messages are a different unit, even when a calculation using them happens to produce the same percentage."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"What overall breach rate does analysis.run report for support_tickets.csv?","options":["10 of 24 tickets, about 41.7%","10 of 12 tickets, about 83.3%","About 26.4%, the mean resolution hours divided by 100"],"correct":0,"explanation":"One row is one completed ticket, so the denominator is all 24 tickets and the numerator is the 10 marked as breached."}]},{"id":"load-and-inspect","title":"2. Load a CSV and check its shape","takeaway":"Inspect columns, row counts and types before transforming a table.","stage":"foundation","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A CSV stores text. pandas infers types when reading it, and inference can hide a wrong assumption. Load with `read_csv`, inspect shape, columns, head and dtypes, then compare them with the data dictionary. Reading successfully does not establish that a file contains the expected records.","Keep the raw input unchanged. Make the source path relative to the script file so the same program works from another directory. A quick preview helps detect a delimiter or header mistake, but examine counts and validation results across the whole table. A clean first five rows cannot certify the rest."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Run this from the practice folder. The file loads as 24 rows and 8 columns with 24 distinct ticket IDs. Reading it with the wrong delimiter still succeeds, but each line lands in a single column: a shape of `(24, 1)` signals a delimiter problem."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"support_tickets.csv\", dtype={\"ticket_id\": \"string\"})\nprint(df.shape)\nprint(df[\"ticket_id\"].nunique())\n\nwrong = pd.read_csv(\"support_tickets.csv\", sep=\";\")\nprint(wrong.shape)\n\n# Output:\n# (24, 8)\n# 24\n# (24, 1)"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A file has 24 rows but only 23 unique ticket IDs. Can you treat it as one row per ticket?","solution":"No. Investigate the repeated ID before reporting ticket-level results.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Compare row count with distinct ticket IDs.","Identify the repeated ID and check whether its values conflict."]},"quiz":{"question":"The first five rows have unique IDs, but the file contains 500 rows. Which check establishes whether IDs are unique throughout?","options":["Compare the full row count with the number of distinct ticket IDs.","Inspect head() again.","Check whether read_csv raised an error."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Uniqueness is a whole-column property. A preview cannot reveal duplicates later in the file, and CSV parsing does not enforce unique business IDs."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"pd.read_csv loads support_tickets.csv. What are its shape and its number of distinct ticket IDs?","options":["24 rows by 8 columns, with 24 distinct IDs","24 rows by 8 columns, with 12 distinct IDs","25 rows by 8 columns, with 24 distinct IDs"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The header is not a row, and matching the row count with the distinct ID count shows one row per ticket throughout the file."}]},{"id":"numpy-arrays","title":"3. Calculate with NumPy arrays","takeaway":"Array operations apply to elements, and shape determines which operations make sense.","stage":"foundation","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A NumPy array stores elements with a shared dtype. `np.array([2, 4, 6], dtype=float)` creates a one-dimensional numeric array of shape `(3,)`. Multiplication by 60 converts every hour value to minutes. A Python list multiplied by 60 repeats its contents instead. Check the container before assuming arithmetic semantics.","For a two-dimensional table, an axis tells a reduction which dimension to collapse. A two-row, three-column array has shape (2, 3). Summing `axis=0` produces one value per column; `axis=1` produces one per row. Broadcasting can combine compatible shapes, but a successful operation may still use the wrong units."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Summing `axis=0` collapses the two rows into one value per column; `axis=1` gives one value per row. NumPy prints arrays without commas. Multiplying an array by 60 converts each value, while multiplying a list repeats it. The length of each result is a useful sanity check."],"example":"import numpy as np\n\ntable = np.array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])\nprint(table.shape)\nprint(table.sum(axis=0))\nprint(table.sum(axis=1))\n\nprint(np.array([2, 4, 6], dtype=float) * 60)\nprint([2, 4] * 2)\n\n# Output:\n# (2, 3)\n# [5 7 9]\n# [ 6 15]\n# [120. 240. 360.]\n# [2, 4, 2, 4]"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Convert np.array([1.5, 2.0]) hours to minutes.","solution":"Multiply by 60 to obtain [90.0, 120.0] minutes.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Keep the output shape at two elements.","Multiply each duration by 60 and label the result in minutes."]},"quiz":{"question":"What does np.array([30.0, 90.0]) / 60 produce when the input is in minutes?","options":["[0.5, 1.5] hours.","[30.0, 90.0] hours.","An array with 120 elements."],"correct":0,"explanation":"NumPy divides each value by 60 and keeps the two-element shape. The numbers change units from minutes to hours."},"references":[{"title":"NumPy beginner guide","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/absolute_beginners.html","section":"Array creation, shape, indexing and basic operations","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"API guidance for the pinned practice libraries; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"What does (df.resolution_hours.to_numpy() > 24).sum() give for the dataset?","options":["10, the same as the breached column's sum","12, because 24 counts as a breach","0.4167, the share of breaches"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A boolean array sums as a count of True values. The strict rule leaves the two 24-hour tickets out, which is why it agrees with the flag."}]},{"id":"types-and-dates","title":"4. Parse values with explicit failures","takeaway":"Convert types deliberately and keep invalid values visible.","stage":"foundation","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Identifiers are labels even if they contain digits. Keep them as strings so leading zeros survive. Convert numeric columns with `to_numeric` and dates with `to_datetime`. `errors=\"raise\"` stops on malformed values; `errors=\"coerce\"` creates missing values. Coercion needs a separate count and review because it can silently change the population used by later summaries.","The supplied `created_date` is a calendar date without a timezone or time of day. It supports grouping by date, but it cannot establish elapsed resolution time. `resolution_hours` is a separate measured outcome in this fictional file. In a real event table, normalize timestamp zones and define which start and end events count before subtracting timestamps."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Coercion turns `\"bad\"` into a missing value and counts one gap; it does not prove the second ticket resolved in zero hours. `errors=\"raise\"` stops instead. Kept as a string, the identifier `007` keeps its leading zeros; converted to a number it becomes 7."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\nraw = pd.Series([\"8\", \"bad\"])\nhours = pd.to_numeric(raw, errors=\"coerce\")\nprint(hours.tolist())\nprint(hours.isna().sum())\n\ntry:\n    pd.to_numeric(raw, errors=\"raise\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"errors='raise' stops at 'bad'\")\n\nids = pd.Series([\"007\"])\nprint(ids.iloc[0], pd.to_numeric(ids).iloc[0])\n\n# Output:\n# [8.0, nan]\n# 1\n# errors='raise' stops at 'bad'\n# 007 7"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A duration cell contains \"unknown\". Should you replace it with zero?","solution":"No. Keep it missing or reject it, and report the missing count.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Distinguish a measured zero from an unavailable value.","Report a missing-value count if numeric conversion coerces the cell."]},"quiz":{"question":"After numeric conversion, durations are [0, 12, missing]. Which report preserves their meaning?","options":["The two measured durations average 6 hours; one duration is missing.","All three tickets average 4 hours.","Zero and missing both mean the ticket was resolved immediately."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The measured zero belongs in the mean. The missing value supplies no duration, so report its absence separately rather than treating it as another zero."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"One created_date is changed to 2026-13-01. What does validate do?","options":["Raises ValueError, because the date does not match %Y-%m-%d","Stores the value as missing","Rolls it over to January 2027"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The date is parsed with an explicit format and errors set to raise, so an impossible month stops the report instead of becoming NaT."}]},{"id":"select-and-filter","title":"5. Select rows without losing the question","takeaway":"Use explicit masks and column names to make the selection reviewable.","stage":"foundation","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Use `df.loc[mask, columns]` to select both rows and columns. A mask is a Boolean Series aligned to the table index. Combine comparisons with `&` or `|` and put each comparison in parentheses. Python `and`/`or` expects single truth values and cannot combine whole pandas Series this way.","State whether your filter changes the population or only the displayed columns. Keeping high-priority tickets changes the population; hiding the team column does not. Record the number of rows before and after a filter. Use `df.loc[mask, \"column\"]` for assignment rather than chained indexing, which has version-dependent copy behavior and is unsupported for reliable updates."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Each comparison sits in its own parentheses and `&` combines the two masks. Of 24 tickets, two are high priority and over 24 hours. Printing the row count before and after the filter shows that it changed the population, not only the columns."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"support_tickets.csv\", dtype={\"ticket_id\": \"string\"})\nmask = (df[\"priority\"] == \"high\") & (df[\"resolution_hours\"] > 24)\nprint(len(df), mask.sum())\nprint(df.loc[mask, [\"ticket_id\", \"resolution_hours\"]].to_string(index=False))\n\n# Output:\n# 24 2\n# ticket_id  resolution_hours\n#      T005                30\n#      T017                32"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A high-priority subset has 3 breaches among 4 tickets. What is its breach rate?","solution":"The subset rate is 3/4 or 75%; it does not describe all tickets.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Use the four high-priority tickets as the denominator.","Keep the conclusion limited to the selected population."]},"quiz":{"question":"A source has 20 tickets. Five are high priority and two of those breached. What can you conclude from that subset?","options":["40% of high-priority tickets breached.","40% of all tickets breached.","10% of high-priority tickets breached."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The selected population contains five tickets, so its rate is 2/5. The overall rate also needs breach information about the other 15 tickets."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/indexing.html","section":"Boolean indexing and selection by label","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"Among high-priority tickets in the dataset, what share breached?","options":["2 of 6, about 33.3%","2 of 24, about 8.3%","10 of 24, about 41.7%"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A rate for a subset uses that subset's own count as the denominator: six high-priority tickets, two of them breached."}]},{"id":"missing-and-duplicates","title":"6. Treat missing values and repeated records separately","takeaway":"Missing values and duplicate rows need different explanations and fixes.","stage":"foundation","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Missing means the value is unavailable. Duplicate means more than one record represents the same intended unit. `isna()` marks unavailable cells with `True`; sum that mask to count them. `duplicated(\"ticket_id\")` tests repeated identifiers. `drop_duplicates()` across all columns removes exact copies, but conflicting records with the same ID need a rule grounded in how the source was collected.","Do not choose the first record just because it makes a test pass. If two versions have explicit update times, the newest validated version may be appropriate. If the source lacks that information, stop and report the conflict. For missing durations, report both total tickets and known durations so readers can see the denominator used by the mean."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Durations 4, missing and 8 have one gap, two known values and a known-value mean of 6. Filling the gap with zero gives a mean of 4, which changes the meaning of the measurement. `duplicated` marks the second `T2` as a repeat of the first."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\nhours = pd.Series([4, None, 8])\nprint(hours.isna().sum(), hours.count(), hours.mean())\nprint(hours.fillna(0).mean())\n\nids = pd.Series([\"T1\", \"T2\", \"T2\"])\nprint(ids.duplicated().tolist())\n\n# Output:\n# 1 2 6.0\n# 4.0\n# [False, False, True]"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Two T009 records contain durations 10 and 30 with no update timestamp. What should the pipeline do?","solution":"Reject or quarantine the conflict for review rather than silently choosing one.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Recognize a conflicting duplicate rather than two independent tickets.","Do not invent an update order that the source does not supply."]},"quiz":{"question":"Two rows for T014 have durations 6 and 9. The source contract says the greatest update timestamp is authoritative, and the 9-hour row is newer. What should you do?","options":["Keep the 9-hour row under the documented rule and record the duplicate handling.","Always keep the first row regardless of timestamps.","Replace both values with their mean of 7.5."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Here the source provides an ordering rule, so the newer record resolves the conflict. Averaging would invent a measurement; file order need not match update order."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/missing_data.html","section":"Detecting missing values and calculations","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"A copy of T001 with a different duration is appended to the data. What does validate do?","options":["Raises ValueError: Ticket IDs must be nonblank and unique","Keeps the later row","Averages the two durations"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The reference has no documented rule for choosing between conflicting records, so it rejects the duplicate instead of picking one silently."}]},{"id":"text-cleaning","title":"7. Clean text columns before you group them","stage":"foundation","takeaway":"Free-text categories split one group into many spellings; normalise them with vectorised .str methods and an explicit mapping, and count what you changed.","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A channel column typed by agents may contain `Email`, `email `, `E-mail` and `EMAIL`. Grouping it directly reports four channels where there is one. The `.str` accessor applies string methods to a whole column at once: `strip` removes surrounding spaces, `lower` or `casefold` fixes case, `replace` with a regular expression collapses repeated whitespace and `extract` pulls a pattern such as a ticket reference into its own column.","Normalising is a decision, not a cleanup chore. Map known variants to canonical values with an explicit dictionary, leave unknown values visible rather than guessing, and report how many rows each rule changed. Missing values stay missing: `.str` methods return missing for missing input, and `str.contains` needs `na=False` when it feeds a filter."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Four spellings collapse to one channel after stripping, lowercasing and mapping. The unknown value `fax` is kept and counted, not hidden. `extract` finds the order reference where one exists and leaves missing values elsewhere."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"channel\": [\"Email\", \" email \", \"E-mail\", \"EMAIL\", \"chat\", \"Chat \", \"fax\", None],\n    \"subject\": [\"Refund ORD-1042\", \"login\", \"ord-77 late\", \"Login\", \"hi\", \"ORD-9 lost\", \"x\", \"y\"],\n})\ncanonical = {\"email\": \"email\", \"e-mail\": \"email\", \"chat\": \"chat\"}\ncleaned = df[\"channel\"].str.strip().str.lower()\ndf[\"channel_clean\"] = cleaned.map(canonical)\nprint(df[\"channel\"].nunique(), \"raw spellings ->\", df[\"channel_clean\"].nunique(), \"channels\")\nprint(\"unknown:\", cleaned[df[\"channel_clean\"].isna() & cleaned.notna()].tolist())\nprint(df[\"channel_clean\"].value_counts().to_dict())\ndf[\"order\"] = df[\"subject\"].str.upper().str.extract(r\"(ORD-\\d+)\", expand=False)\nprint(df[\"order\"].tolist())\nprint(df[\"subject\"].str.contains(\"login\", case=False, na=False).sum())\n\n# Output:\n# 7 raw spellings -> 2 channels\n# unknown: ['fax']\n# {'email': 4, 'chat': 2}\n# ['ORD-1042', nan, 'ORD-77', nan, nan, 'ORD-9', nan, nan]\n# 2"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A `priority` column contains `High`, `high `, `HIGH`, `hi`, `Urgent`, `low` and missing values. Write the cleaning steps, decide what happens to `hi` and `Urgent`, and say what you report alongside the cleaned counts.","solution":"Strip and lowercase with .str.strip().str.lower(), then map through an explicit dictionary such as {'high': 'high', 'low': 'low'}. Whether 'hi' means high and whether 'urgent' is a separate level or an alias of high must be confirmed with the data owner, not guessed; until then keep them as unmapped values. Report the number of rows changed by each step, the unmapped values with counts, and the missing values separately.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Strips and normalises case with vectorised .str methods.","Uses an explicit mapping and does not guess ambiguous values.","Reports changed, unmapped and missing counts."]},"quiz":{"question":"Why pass `na=False` to `str.contains` when building a filter?","options":["It makes the match case-insensitive","Missing text would otherwise produce missing values in the mask instead of False","It removes missing rows from the table"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A boolean filter needs True or False for every row. na=False treats missing text as not matching."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide: Working with text data","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/text.html","section":"String methods; extracting substrings","reviewed":"2026-11-08","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"In the lesson's example, how many raw channel spellings are there before cleaning?","options":["4","7","8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Email, ' email ', E-mail, EMAIL, chat, 'Chat ' and fax are seven distinct non-missing values; nunique ignores the missing one."}]},{"id":"indexes-and-alignment","title":"8. Understand labels and row positions","stage":"foundation","takeaway":"Pandas aligns labeled values by index, which can differ from row position.","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A Series has values and an index. Adding two Series matches index labels rather than blindly adding the first item to the first item. This is useful when rows have been reordered, but it can create missing results when labels do not match. Use iloc for positional selection and loc for label selection.","Inspect the index after filtering or sorting. A filtered table may retain labels 2 and 5 rather than becoming labels 0 and 1. Resetting the index changes those labels; it does not change the underlying ticket identities. Keep `ticket_id` as the business identifier and choose positional array arithmetic only when you have verified both row orders."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["pandas pairs values by index label, so A gets 10 + 2 and B gets 20 + 1. Converting both Series to NumPy arrays pairs them by position instead. Adding a Series with no matching labels produces missing values for all three labels."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\nleft = pd.Series([10, 20], index=[\"A\", \"B\"])\nright = pd.Series([1, 2], index=[\"B\", \"A\"])\nprint((left + right).to_dict())\nprint(left.to_numpy() + right.to_numpy())\n\nunmatched = left + pd.Series([5], index=[\"C\"])\nprint(unmatched.isna().sum())\n\n# Output:\n# {'A': 12, 'B': 21}\n# [11 22]\n# 3"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A filtered table has index labels 2 and 5. How do you select its first stored row?","solution":"Use iloc[0]. loc[0] looks for label 0 and may fail.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Choose positional selection for the first stored row.","Explain why label 0 may not exist after filtering."]},"quiz":{"question":"A table has unique index labels 2 and 5, in that order. Which expression selects the row labeled 5?","options":["df.loc[5]","df.iloc[5]","df.loc[1]"],"correct":0,"explanation":"loc selects the label 5. iloc[5] requests the sixth stored row, which this two-row table does not have. The second row does not acquire label 1 automatically."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/indexing.html","section":"Selection by label and position","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"high = df[df.priority == 'high']. What does high.iloc[1].ticket_id return?","options":["T005","T002","A KeyError"],"correct":0,"explanation":"iloc counts positions in the filtered table, whose second row is T005. Its index label is still 4, from the original table."}]},{"id":"derive-and-validate","title":"9. Derive columns with units and boundary checks","takeaway":"A derived column must implement a stated rule, including equality at its boundary.","stage":"intermediate","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["The teaching rule defines a breach as `resolution_hours > 24`. Exactly 24 hours does not breach. Derive the flag from validated durations and compare it with the supplied breached column. A disagreement can reveal a changed policy, corrupted export or wrong operator. Avoid overwriting the original flag before checking it.","Vectorized operations make the same rule apply across rows, but they do not validate inputs automatically. Reject negative or nonfinite durations before calculating a flag. A numeric conversion can still accept infinity. Keep the rule in a named function and test values below, at and above the threshold."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Exactly 24 hours does not breach, because the rule is strictly greater than 24. Converting to minutes changes the units but not which cases breach, as long as the threshold also becomes 1440 minutes."],"example":"import numpy as np\n\nhours = np.array([23.9, 24.0, 24.1])\nprint(hours > 24)\n\nminutes = hours * 60\nprint(minutes > 24 * 60)\n\n# Output:\n# [False False  True]\n# [False False  True]"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Does a duration of exactly 24 hours breach this rule?","solution":"No. The rule uses greater than 24, not greater than or equal to 24.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Write the exact comparison used by the rule.","Check the boundary value without rounding it into another case."]},"quiz":{"question":"The rule changes to resolution_hours >= 24. How many of [23, 24, 25] breach?","options":["Two.","One.","Three."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The inclusive comparison counts 24 and 25. Changing > to >= changes the boundary case, so the derived column and its tests must follow the stated rule."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"T004 has resolution_hours 24. Its breached flag is changed to 1. What does validate do?","options":["Raises ValueError: Breach flag disagrees with strict 24-hour rule","Accepts it, because 24 hours is a breach","Corrects the flag to 0"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The flag is checked against resolution_hours > 24, so a stored value that disagrees with the rule is rejected rather than repaired."}]},{"id":"vectorize-not-apply","title":"10. Prefer vectorised operations to row-wise apply","stage":"intermediate","takeaway":"Whole-column operations run in compiled code; row-wise apply calls Python once per row. Use vectorised arithmetic, np.where and np.select, and keep apply for logic that cannot be expressed on columns.","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["`df.apply(f, axis=1)` builds a Series for each row and calls a Python function on it, so its cost grows with Python overhead per row. Column arithmetic, comparisons, `.str` and `.dt` methods operate on whole arrays in compiled code. For conditional values use `np.where` for two outcomes and `np.select` for several, with conditions checked in order and an explicit default.","Vectorised code is not only faster; it states the rule once for the column, which makes boundary checks (lesson 9) easier to test. Keep apply (or a plain loop) for logic that genuinely needs per-row Python, such as calling a parser that has no vectorised form, and measure before optimising anything else."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Both versions assign the same service level to 100,000 tickets. The row-wise version is many times slower; the exact ratio depends on the machine, so the example prints only whether the vectorised version was at least five times faster."],"example":"import time\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(7)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"hours\": rng.integers(0, 100, 100_000),\n                   \"priority\": rng.choice([\"high\", \"low\"], 100_000)})\n\ndef level(row):\n    if row[\"priority\"] == \"high\" and row[\"hours\"] > 8:\n        return \"breach\"\n    if row[\"hours\"] > 48:\n        return \"breach\"\n    return \"ok\" if row[\"hours\"] <= 24 else \"late\"\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\nby_row = df.apply(level, axis=1)\nrow_seconds = time.perf_counter() - start\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\nbreach = ((df[\"priority\"] == \"high\") & (df[\"hours\"] > 8)) | (df[\"hours\"] > 48)\nby_column = pd.Series(np.select([breach, df[\"hours\"] <= 24], [\"breach\", \"ok\"], default=\"late\"))\ncolumn_seconds = time.perf_counter() - start\n\nprint(by_row.equals(by_column), by_column.value_counts().sort_index().to_dict())\nprint(\"vectorised at least 5x faster:\", row_seconds > 5 * column_seconds)\n\n# Output:\n# True {'breach': 71283, 'late': 11868, 'ok': 16849}\n# vectorised at least 5x faster: True"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A report labels each ticket `quick` (under 2 hours), `normal` (2 to 24 hours) or `slow` (over 24 hours), and missing durations must stay unlabelled. The current code uses apply with if statements and takes minutes on a large file. Rewrite the rule with vectorised operations and say how you would show the result is unchanged.","solution":"Use np.select with conditions [hours < 2, hours <= 24, hours > 24] and choices ['quick', 'normal', 'slow'], with default set to a missing value so missing durations, which fail every comparison, stay unlabelled (or pd.cut with bins [0, 2, 24, inf] and right edges chosen to match the rule exactly). Check boundaries 2 and 24 explicitly. Show equivalence by running both versions on the same sample, including boundary and missing values, and asserting the Series are equal before replacing the old code.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Expresses the rule with np.select or pd.cut on whole columns.","Handles the 2 and 24 hour boundaries and missing durations.","Proves equivalence by comparing old and new results on the same data."]},"quiz":{"question":"Why is `df.apply(f, axis=1)` usually slow on large tables?","options":["It sorts the table first","It calls a Python function once per row instead of operating on whole columns in compiled code","It copies the file from disk"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Per-row Python calls dominate the cost. Column operations run once over arrays."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide: Enhancing performance","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/enhancingperf.html","section":"Cython (writing C extensions for pandas); vectorisation advice","reviewed":"2026-11-08","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."},{"title":"NumPy: numpy.select","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.select.html","section":"numpy.select","reviewed":"2026-11-08","scope":"NumPy 2.5 API; the practice environment pins its own NumPy version."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"With np.select, a ticket matches both the first and the second condition. Which choice does it get?","options":["The first matching condition's choice","The last matching condition's choice","The default"],"correct":0,"explanation":"np.select checks conditions in order and uses the first that is true for each element."}]},{"id":"group-and-denominators","title":"11. Summarize groups with counts","takeaway":"Show the group denominator beside its rate.","stage":"intermediate","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["groupby splits rows by a key and computes an aggregate for each group. size counts rows; count on a column counts its nonmissing values. For validated zero-or-one breach flags, mean is the fraction of ones. Include ticket count, breach count and mean duration so the reader can check the rate and see how much data supports it.","A mean of group rates gives every group equal weight. An overall ticket rate must weight groups by ticket count. This difference matters when teams handle different volumes. Missing group labels also need a policy because `groupby` normally excludes missing keys; use `dropna=False` when auditing unclassified rows."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Team A has 1 breach in 2 tickets and team B has 1 in 8. The unweighted mean of their rates is 31.25%. The overall ticket rate is 2/10, or 20%, because it weights each team by its tickets."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"team\": [\"A\"] * 2 + [\"B\"] * 8,\n                   \"breached\": [1, 0] + [1] + [0] * 7})\nsummary = df.groupby(\"team\").agg(tickets=(\"breached\", \"size\"),\n                                 breaches=(\"breached\", \"sum\"),\n                                 rate=(\"breached\", \"mean\"))\nprint(summary)\nprint(summary[\"rate\"].mean())\nprint(df[\"breached\"].mean())\n\n# Output:\n#       tickets  breaches   rate\n# team\n# A           2         1  0.500\n# B           8         1  0.125\n# 0.3125\n# 0.2"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Which overall rate should describe all ten tickets in the example?","solution":"Use 2/10 or 20%, because each ticket gets equal weight.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Add breach counts across both groups.","Add ticket counts before calculating the overall fraction."]},"quiz":{"question":"Team A has 1 breach among 2 tickets; team B has 3 among 18. What is the overall ticket breach rate?","options":["4/20, or 20%.","The unweighted mean of 50% and 16.7%, about 33.3%.","3/18, about 16.7%."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Add the four breaches and all 20 tickets before dividing. Averaging team rates equally gives a small team too much weight; using only team B drops team A."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/groupby.html","section":"Aggregation and missing group keys","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"visual":{"title":"Split tickets by team, then weight the rate","intro":"Follow ten tickets through groupby, then compare a ticket-weighted rate with an average of team rates.","scope":"Fictional tickets from the lesson's worked example: team A has 1 breach in 2 tickets and team B 1 in 8. Breach flags are already validated as 0 or 1.","sources":[{"title":"pandas user guide: Group by: split-apply-combine","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/groupby.html"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"weighted","label":"Weight by tickets","steps":[{"title":"Ten ticket rows","explanation":"Each row is one ticket with its team and a breach flag of 0 or 1. Two tickets breached: T1 in team A and T3 in team B.","nodes":[{"id":"rows","label":"Ticket rows","detail":"10 rows: team and breach flag (0 or 1)","x":40,"y":50,"tone":"active"},{"id":"split","label":"groupby(\"team\")","detail":"Not applied yet","x":390,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"all","label":"Overall rate","detail":"Not calculated yet","x":740,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"}],"edges":[{"from":"rows","to":"split","label":"split by team"},{"from":"split","to":"all","label":"combine"}]},{"title":"Split by team","focus":["group-A","group-B"],"explanation":"groupby(\"team\") puts every row in its team's group: 2 rows for team A and 8 for team B. No row is copied or dropped.","nodes":[{"id":"rows","label":"Ticket rows","detail":"Each row goes to exactly one group","x":40,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"split","label":"groupby(\"team\")","detail":"A: 2 rows · B: 8 rows","x":390,"y":50,"tone":"active"},{"id":"all","label":"Overall rate","detail":"Not calculated yet","x":740,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"}],"edges":[{"from":"rows","to":"split","label":"split by team"},{"from":"split","to":"all","label":"combine"}]},{"title":"Aggregate each group","focus":["agg-A","agg-B"],"explanation":"size counts each team's tickets, sum counts its breaches, and the mean of the 0/1 flags is its breach rate. Show the count beside each rate: team A's 50% rests on only 2 tickets.","nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Team A","detail":"1 breach in 2 tickets: 50%","x":40,"y":50,"tone":"active"},{"id":"b","label":"Team B","detail":"1 breach in 8 tickets: 12.5%","x":390,"y":50,"tone":"active"},{"id":"all","label":"Overall rate","detail":"Not calculated yet","x":740,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"all","label":"breaches and tickets"},{"from":"b","to":"all","label":"breaches and tickets"}],"tables":[{"caption":"Per-team summary","columns":["Team","Tickets (size)","Breaches (sum)","Breach rate (mean)"],"rows":[["A","2","1","50%"],["B","8","1","12.5%"]]}]},{"title":"Combine with ticket weights","focus":["overall"],"explanation":"Add the breaches and the tickets across teams before dividing: 2 / 10 = 20%. Every ticket counts once, so each team counts in proportion to its tickets.","nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Team A","detail":"1 breach in 2 tickets: 50%","x":40,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"b","label":"Team B","detail":"1 breach in 8 tickets: 12.5%","x":390,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"all","label":"Overall ticket rate","detail":"2 breaches / 10 tickets = 20%","x":740,"y":50,"tone":"success"}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"all","label":"breaches and tickets"},{"from":"b","to":"all","label":"breaches and tickets"}],"tables":[{"caption":"Per-team summary","columns":["Team","Tickets (size)","Breaches (sum)","Breach rate (mean)"],"rows":[["A","2","1","50%"],["B","8","1","12.5%"]]}]}]},{"id":"unweighted","label":"Average the team rates","steps":[{"title":"Two team rates","explanation":"The per-team summary is the same as before: 50% for team A and 12.5% for team B.","nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Team A","detail":"1 breach in 2 tickets: 50%","x":40,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"b","label":"Team B","detail":"1 breach in 8 tickets: 12.5%","x":390,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"all","label":"Overall rate","detail":"Not calculated yet","x":740,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"all","label":"team rate"},{"from":"b","to":"all","label":"team rate"}],"tables":[{"caption":"Per-team summary","columns":["Team","Tickets (size)","Breaches (sum)","Breach rate (mean)"],"rows":[["A","2","1","50%"],["B","8","1","12.5%"]]}]},{"title":"Wrong: average the two rates","focus":["overall"],"explanation":"Averaging the two rates gives each team equal weight, so team A's 2 tickets count as much as team B's 8. The result is 31.25%, although only 2 of the 10 tickets breached.","nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Team A","detail":"1 breach in 2 tickets: 50%","x":40,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"b","label":"Team B","detail":"1 breach in 8 tickets: 12.5%","x":390,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"all","label":"Mean of team rates","detail":"(50% + 12.5%) / 2 = 31.25%","x":740,"y":50,"tone":"warning"}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"all","label":"team rate"},{"from":"b","to":"all","label":"team rate"}],"tables":[{"caption":"Per-team summary","columns":["Team","Tickets (size)","Breaches (sum)","Breach rate (mean)"],"rows":[["A","2","1","50%"],["B","8","1","12.5%"]]}]},{"title":"Right: count the tickets","focus":["overall"],"explanation":"The overall ticket rate divides total breaches by total tickets: 2 / 10 = 20%. A mean of team rates answers a different question, about the typical team, and needs that label.","nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Team A","detail":"1 breach in 2 tickets: 50%","x":40,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"b","label":"Team B","detail":"1 breach in 8 tickets: 12.5%","x":390,"y":50,"tone":"neutral"},{"id":"all","label":"Overall ticket rate","detail":"2 breaches / 10 tickets = 20%","x":740,"y":50,"tone":"success"}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"all","label":"breaches and tickets"},{"from":"b","to":"all","label":"breaches and tickets"}],"tables":[{"caption":"Per-team summary","columns":["Team","Tickets (size)","Breaches (sum)","Breach rate (mean)"],"rows":[["A","2","1","50%"],["B","8","1","12.5%"]]}]}]}]},"extraQuiz":[{"question":"What breach rates does summarize report for billing and technical?","options":["Billing about 33.3%, technical 50%","Billing 40%, technical 60%, sharing the 10 breaches","Billing about 16.7%, technical 25%, out of all 24"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each team's rate uses its own 12 tickets: 4 breaches for billing and 6 for technical."}]},{"id":"joins-and-cardinality","title":"12. Join lookup tables without multiplying tickets","takeaway":"Validate the expected relationship before accepting a join.","stage":"intermediate","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A left merge can attach a team owner to every ticket. The ticket table contains many rows for each team; the lookup must contain one row for each team. `merge(..., validate=\"many_to_one\")` rejects duplicate lookup keys. A duplicate right-side key otherwise repeats matching ticket rows and changes later counts and sums.","Cardinality validation does not prove every key matched. Use `indicator=True` and inspect `left_only` rows. Decide whether an unknown team should stop the report or appear as unassigned. pandas matches null join keys to each other, unlike the usual SQL null comparison, so reject missing required keys rather than assuming SQL behavior."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Two billing tickets merged with two billing owner rows produce four rows. Declaring the merge many-to-one rejects that lookup before any count or sum is computed."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ntickets = pd.DataFrame({\"ticket_id\": [\"T1\", \"T2\"],\n                        \"team\": [\"billing\", \"billing\"]})\nowners = pd.DataFrame({\"team\": [\"billing\", \"billing\"],\n                       \"owner\": [\"Asha\", \"Ravi\"]})\nprint(len(tickets.merge(owners, on=\"team\", how=\"left\")))\n\ntry:\n    tickets.merge(owners, on=\"team\", how=\"left\", validate=\"many_to_one\")\nexcept pd.errors.MergeError:\n    print(\"MergeError: billing appears twice in the lookup\")\n\n# Output:\n# 4\n# MergeError: billing appears twice in the lookup"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A lookup has two billing rows with different owners. Is a left join alone enough?","solution":"No. Enforce many-to-one cardinality and investigate the duplicate lookup key.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Identify team as the right-side key that must be unique.","Check cardinality before accepting the merged table."]},"quiz":{"question":"Three tickets have team billing. The lookup contains two billing rows. How many rows does an ordinary left join produce for those tickets?","options":["Six, because each ticket matches both lookup rows.","Three, because a left join always preserves the left row count.","Two, because only the lookup rows count."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each of the three left rows has two matches, yielding six rows. A many-to-one validation would reject this lookup before inflated totals are accepted."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/merging.html","section":"Merge key uniqueness and indicator","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"diagram":{"title":"Why a duplicate lookup multiplies tickets","summary":"Each left row pairs with every matching right row. A two-row billing lookup doubles the two billing tickets.","type":"flow","nodes":[{"id":"tickets","label":"Two billing tickets","description":"T001 and T003 each represent one completed ticket."},{"id":"lookup","label":"Two billing owners","description":"The lookup incorrectly contains both Asha and Sam for the same team key."},{"id":"merge","label":"Four matched rows","description":"Each of the two tickets pairs with each owner, producing four rows."},{"id":"validate","label":"Reject many-to-one violation","description":"validate=\"many_to_one\" detects that billing is duplicated on the right. Fix the lookup before reporting."}],"edges":[{"from":"tickets","to":"merge","label":"two left matches"},{"from":"lookup","to":"merge","label":"two right matches per ticket"},{"from":"lookup","to":"validate","label":"check key uniqueness first"}],"steps":[{"title":"Inspect the input grain","activeNodes":["tickets","lookup"],"activeEdges":[],"explanation":"The left side is one row per ticket. The intended lookup should have one row per team."},{"title":"Follow every match","activeNodes":["tickets","lookup","merge"],"activeEdges":[0,1],"explanation":"Two tickets multiplied by two lookup records produces four output rows."},{"title":"Stop before aggregation","activeNodes":["lookup","validate"],"activeEdges":[2],"explanation":"Cardinality validation rejects the duplicate team key. Row counts alone cannot tell which owner is correct."}]},"extraQuiz":[{"question":"teams.csv gains a second billing row. What does attach_owners do?","options":["Raises MergeError: the lookup is not many-to-one","Doubles every billing ticket","Keeps the first owner"],"correct":0,"explanation":"validate='many_to_one' checks the lookup's keys before joining, so a duplicate cannot multiply ticket rows."}]},{"id":"distributions-and-outliers","title":"13. Read a distribution before choosing one average","takeaway":"An unusual value needs investigation, and one average can hide its effect.","stage":"intermediate","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Use count, minimum, maximum, median and mean together before interpreting a duration column. A long right tail can pull the mean above most observations. The median identifies the middle of sorted observations, while the mean retains the contribution of every duration. Neither describes all features of the distribution.","A long duration may be a real difficult ticket, a unit error or a missing closure event. Check the source before removing it. Show results with and without a questioned value only when you explain the exclusion rule. Deleting every inconvenient observation makes the report look stable by changing the question."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["For durations 2, 3, 4, 5 and 86, the mean is 20 hours and the median is 4. Both are correct. The 86-hour case accounts for 86% of the total waiting time."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\nhours = pd.Series([2, 3, 4, 5, 86])\nprint(hours.mean(), hours.median())\nprint(round(86 / hours.sum(), 2))\n\n# Output:\n# 20.0 4.0\n# 0.86"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Which summary reveals the long tail in those five durations?","solution":"Report mean 20, median 4 and maximum 86 with the count of 5.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Calculate the sum as 100 and divide by five for the mean.","Identify the third sorted value as the median.","Keep the maximum visible while its validity is investigated."]},"quiz":{"question":"Durations are [2, 3, 4, 5, 86]. The source confirms that 86 is valid. What should the summary do?","options":["Retain it and report the tail alongside typical durations.","Delete it because it is larger than the mean.","Replace it with the median without recording a change."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A valid long duration is part of the distribution. The median helps describe a typical observation, while the maximum and mean keep the long tail visible."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"What are the mean and median of resolution_hours in the dataset?","options":["Mean about 26.4 hours, median 22 hours","Mean 22 hours, median about 26.4 hours","Both 24 hours"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A few long tickets, up to 96 hours, pull the mean above the median. Report both and the tail rather than one average."}]},{"id":"correlation-and-outliers","title":"14. Measure association and flag outliers with explicit rules","stage":"intermediate","takeaway":"Pearson correlation measures linear association and is pulled by a few extreme values; Spearman uses ranks. Flag outliers with a stated rule such as the IQR fences and report results with and without them.","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A correlation coefficient summarises how two columns move together, from -1 to 1. Pearson's coefficient measures linear association and can be dominated by one extreme point. Spearman's coefficient correlates ranks, so it measures any monotonic relationship and resists outliers. Neither says one variable causes the other (lesson 18), and a near-zero value can hide a strong curved relationship, so plot the data first (lesson 16).","Lesson 13 asked whether a long duration is real before excluding it. To flag candidates consistently, use a stated rule: the interquartile range (IQR) fences mark values below Q1 - 1.5 × IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. Flag them in a column, investigate, and show the result with and without them; do not delete rows silently."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Without the extreme ticket, replies and hours are strongly associated by both measures. One ticket with 400 hours drags Pearson's coefficient from 0.98 to 0.64, while Spearman stays at 0.99 because the ticket is still simply the largest rank. The IQR rule flags exactly that ticket."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"replies\": [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7],\n                   \"hours\":   [2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 12, 400]})\nclean = df.iloc[:-1]\nprint(\"without outlier:\", round(clean.corr().loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2),\n      round(clean.corr(method=\"spearman\").loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2))\nprint(\"with outlier:   \", round(df.corr().loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2),\n      round(df.corr(method=\"spearman\").loc[\"replies\", \"hours\"], 2))\n\nq1, q3 = df[\"hours\"].quantile([0.25, 0.75])\niqr = q3 - q1\ndf[\"outlier\"] = (df[\"hours\"] < q1 - 1.5 * iqr) | (df[\"hours\"] > q3 + 1.5 * iqr)\nprint(q1, q3, df.loc[df[\"outlier\"], \"hours\"].tolist())\n\n# Output:\n# without outlier: 0.98 0.99\n# with outlier:    0.64 0.99\n# 4.5 9.75 [400]"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A manager reads a Pearson correlation of 0.05 between ticket length (characters) and resolution hours and concludes that length does not matter. List what you would check before agreeing and how you would report the result.","solution":"Plot length against hours to look for a curved or clustered relationship that Pearson misses. Compute Spearman as well. Flag outliers with the IQR rule and recompute both coefficients with and without them, because a few extreme tickets can hide or create an association. Check groups (priority, channel) separately, since mixing groups can cancel a relationship. Report the coefficients with their method and row count, the outlier rule and its effect, and state that correlation describes association in this data, not whether making tickets shorter would change resolution time.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Plots the data and compares Pearson with Spearman.","Tests sensitivity to flagged outliers with a stated rule.","Checks subgroups and avoids a causal conclusion."]},"quiz":{"question":"Which coefficient is less affected by a single extreme value?","options":["Pearson","Spearman","Both are affected equally"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Spearman uses ranks, so an extreme value is only the largest rank, not a huge distance."},"references":[{"title":"pandas API: DataFrame.corr","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.corr.html","section":"method: pearson, kendall, spearman","reviewed":"2026-11-08","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."},{"title":"pandas API: Series.quantile","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.Series.quantile.html","section":"interpolation","reviewed":"2026-11-08","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"In the lesson's example, what happens to Pearson's coefficient when the 400-hour ticket is included?","options":["It stays at 0.98","It falls to 0.64","It becomes negative"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The single extreme point dominates the linear measure, while Spearman, based on ranks, stays at 0.99."}]},{"id":"dates-and-aggregation","title":"15. Group dates without inventing observations","takeaway":"A date with no rows is different from a measured zero.","stage":"intermediate","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Parse dates, sort them and choose a reporting interval before resampling. A daily ticket count measures tickets created each day; a daily mean duration groups their eventual outcomes by creation day. It is not the same as tickets resolved that day. Explain which event establishes the date group.","An absent date may mean no activity or an incomplete export. Reindexing a date range can reveal gaps, but filling them with zero requires evidence that collection was complete. For an average duration on a day with no completed tickets, zero is usually misleading; there is no observed duration to average."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Three tickets created on Monday resolve on Tuesday and Wednesday. Grouping by creation date places all three on Monday. A closure workload chart would need the resolution dates instead."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"created_date\": pd.to_datetime([\"2026-01-05\"] * 3),\n    \"resolved_date\": pd.to_datetime([\"2026-01-06\", \"2026-01-06\",\n                                     \"2026-01-07\"]),\n})\nprint(df[\"created_date\"].dt.day_name().value_counts().to_dict())\nprint(df[\"resolved_date\"].dt.day_name().value_counts().to_dict())\n\n# Output:\n# {'Monday': 3}\n# {'Tuesday': 2, 'Wednesday': 1}"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"The export has no Tuesday rows and its completeness is unknown. What should a daily chart show?","solution":"Mark Tuesday as missing or explain the gap until collection completeness is established.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Separate an absent observation from verified zero activity.","State which event date defines the chart."]},"quiz":{"question":"Collection is confirmed complete for Wednesday, and no tickets were opened that day. What belongs in the daily opened-ticket count?","options":["Zero, with the completeness assumption recorded.","A missing value in every case.","The count from Tuesday."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Confirmed complete collection supports a measured count of zero. This differs from an unexplained gap, where absence of rows alone cannot establish zero activity."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"Tickets are counted per week ending on Sunday. How many fall in the week ending 2026-01-04?","options":["4, because the data starts on Thursday 1 January","7","0"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The first week is partial: the data begins mid-week. Say so instead of comparing it with full weeks."}]},{"id":"charts-for-questions","title":"16. Choose a chart for the comparison","takeaway":"Label the measure and units, and keep the plot consistent with the table.","stage":"intermediate","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Use a bar chart for counts by team, a histogram for a duration distribution and a line chart for an ordered time series. These forms answer different questions. Matplotlib separates a Figure, the output container, from an Axes, where data and labels appear. The explicit `fig, ax = plt.subplots()` style helps when adding labels or several plots.","A bar chart compares lengths, so a zero baseline usually matters. Label hours as hours and rates as fractions or percentages consistently. A chart needs a text summary and accessible labels; color alone should not encode the conclusion. Keep categories in a stable order so two generated reports can be compared."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["The bar chart puts team on the horizontal axis and ticket count on the vertical axis, with a zero baseline. The kit's report saves this chart as `counts.png` and repeats the numeric counts, so the image is not the only way to read them."],"example":"import matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport pandas as pd\n\ncounts = pd.read_csv(\"support_tickets.csv\").groupby(\"team\").size()\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.bar(counts.index, counts.to_numpy())\nax.set(xlabel=\"Team\", ylabel=\"Ticket count\")\nprint(ax.get_xlabel(), \"|\", ax.get_ylabel(), \"| baseline\", ax.get_ylim()[0])\nprint(counts.to_dict())\nplt.close(fig)\n\n# Output:\n# Team | Ticket count | baseline 0.0\n# {'billing': 12, 'technical': 12}"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Which chart best shows the shape of individual resolution durations?","solution":"Use a histogram of resolution_hours with labeled hour bins and ticket counts.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Put duration in hours on the histogram axis.","Use ticket frequency for the bin counts."]},"quiz":{"question":"You want to compare ticket counts across four teams. Which chart fits that question?","options":["A bar chart with one bar per team and a labeled ticket-count axis.","A line through arbitrary ticket IDs.","A histogram of resolution hours."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Bars compare counts across named groups. A duration histogram answers a distribution question, and arbitrary ticket IDs do not define a time series."},"references":[{"title":"Matplotlib quick start","url":"https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/explain/quick_start.html","section":"Figure, Axes and explicit plotting interface","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"API guidance for the pinned practice libraries; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"What does run() draw in counts.png?","options":["One bar per team, showing ticket counts on a labelled axis","A histogram of resolution hours","A line of tickets per day"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The chart answers the team comparison. Each bar is labelled with its count, and the distribution belongs in a separate histogram."}]},{"id":"reshape-for-comparison","title":"17. Reshape after checking unique combinations","stage":"intermediate","takeaway":"A pivot needs a defined value for every row-and-column combination.","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A long table stores a category in a column; a wide table gives selected categories their own columns. A team-by-priority count table can make comparisons easier. Group and count first, then unstack or pivot the summary. The reshaping step changes presentation, not what counts as one ticket.","`pivot` requires unique index-and-column combinations. Repeated team-priority rows in a raw ticket table are expected, so a direct pivot of duration can fail. `pivot_table` can aggregate them, but specify the aggregation rather than accepting a mean that may answer the wrong question. Filling missing count combinations with zero needs confidence that the input population is complete."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Billing has two low-priority tickets and one high-priority ticket. Counting first and then unstacking produces one billing row with `low` 2 and `high` 1. A direct `pivot` of durations fails because billing and low occur together twice. A sum of `resolution_hours` would measure something else."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"team\": [\"billing\"] * 3,\n                   \"priority\": [\"low\", \"low\", \"high\"],\n                   \"resolution_hours\": [4, 6, 30]})\nprint(df.groupby([\"team\", \"priority\"]).size().unstack(fill_value=0))\n\ntry:\n    df.pivot(index=\"team\", columns=\"priority\", values=\"resolution_hours\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"pivot fails: billing and low appear together twice\")\n\n# Output:\n# priority  high  low\n# team\n# billing      1    2\n# pivot fails: billing and low appear together twice"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Several tickets share the same team and priority. How do you build a count table?","solution":"Group by team and priority, count tickets, then reshape the resulting counts.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Count at the team-priority combination before reshaping.","Keep duration measures separate from frequency counts."]},"quiz":{"question":"After grouping, billing has 3 high-priority and 2 low-priority tickets. What should the billing row contain after reshaping the counts?","options":["High: 3, low: 2, with a row total of 5.","High: 1, low: 1, because there are two groups.","The mean duration of each priority."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Reshaping moves the existing counts into columns. It does not change their meaning or replace ticket counts with durations."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/reshaping.html","section":"Pivot and pivot tables","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"pd.crosstab(df.team, df.channel) counts the dataset. What does billing's row show?","options":["Chat 4, email 8","Chat 8, email 4","Chat 6, email 6"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each team and channel pair is one cell, and the row adds up to billing's 12 tickets."}]},{"id":"association-and-comparison","title":"18. Compare groups without claiming a cause","takeaway":"A difference between groups does not establish why it occurred.","stage":"advanced","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["An email-versus-chat comparison can describe the rows, but customers choose channels and teams may route difficult work differently. A channel difference may reflect case mix, priority or staffing. Split a descriptive table by priority to see whether a broad pattern persists, but remember that unrecorded differences can remain.","The dataset is fictional and small. Its patterns exist to test code, not to measure a service or estimate a treatment effect. Avoid p-values or causal claims that imply a sampling design the file does not have. A useful conclusion states the observed difference, its denominator and the additional evidence needed for a decision."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Email averages 24 hours and chat 12. Within each priority, the two channels take the same time: email simply carries more high-priority tickets. Switching everyone to chat is not a justified recommendation, because the data does not show what would happen to the same cases under a different channel."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"channel\":  [\"email\"] * 4 + [\"chat\"] * 4,\n    \"priority\": [\"high\", \"high\", \"high\", \"low\", \"high\", \"low\", \"low\", \"low\"],\n    \"hours\":    [30, 30, 30, 6, 30, 6, 6, 6],\n})\nprint(df.groupby(\"channel\")[\"hours\"].mean().to_dict())\nprint(df.pivot_table(index=\"priority\", columns=\"channel\",\n                     values=\"hours\", aggfunc=\"mean\"))\n\n# Output:\n# {'chat': 12.0, 'email': 24.0}\n# channel   chat  email\n# priority\n# high      30.0   30.0\n# low        6.0    6.0"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Email tickets are slower in this file. Does that prove email causes delays?","solution":"No. Describe the difference and investigate case mix or a suitable study before claiming causation.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Describe the observed association without changing it into a causal claim.","Name at least one possible difference in case mix."]},"quiz":{"question":"Email has more complex tickets than chat. Its average resolution time is also higher. Which next comparison helps investigate case mix?","options":["Compare channels within similar complexity groups, while checking for other differences.","Conclude that switching every ticket to chat will reduce its duration.","Remove complexity from the data because it complicates the chart."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Comparing similar cases can help examine a measured difference in case mix. It still does not establish causation or rule out unmeasured differences between channels."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."},{"title":"OpenStax experimental design","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/1-4-experimental-design-and-ethics","section":"Observational studies, random assignment and confounding","scope":"Study-design context for why a grouped descriptive comparison does not by itself establish causation.","reviewed":"2026-10-02"}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"pd.crosstab(df.team, df.priority) is run on the dataset. How are the high-priority tickets spread?","options":["All 6 are billing tickets, so team and priority are entangled","3 billing and 3 technical","None are billing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A team comparison here is also a priority comparison. The sample cannot separate the two, and neither difference shows a cause."}]},{"id":"validation-functions","title":"19. Make invalid input fail before reporting","takeaway":"Check the data contract before creating summaries or charts.","stage":"advanced","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A validation function should check required columns, unique nonblank IDs, allowed categories, parseable dates, nonnegative finite durations and consistent breach flags. A clean happy-path fixture is not enough. Construct broken cases to prove each relevant check actually rejects bad input.","Decide whether extra columns are acceptable and document the decision. The kit requires its eight columns but permits extras, allowing a future source to add metadata without changing the calculations. It rejects missing values in required fields. A more permissive real pipeline would need explicit missing-value and quarantine policies."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["This uses the kit's `validate` function. The supplied file is accepted. A negative duration, a repeated ID and an unknown team are each rejected with `ValueError`. A report that prints plausible totals despite those changes has failed its input contract."],"example":"import pandas as pd\nfrom analysis import validate\n\ndef rejection(frame):\n    try:\n        validate(frame)\n    except ValueError as error:\n        return str(error)\n    return \"accepted\"\n\ngood = pd.read_csv(\"support_tickets.csv\", dtype={\"ticket_id\": \"string\"})\nprint(rejection(good))\n\nnegative = good.copy()\nnegative.loc[0, \"resolution_hours\"] = -1\nprint(rejection(negative))\n\nduplicate = good.copy()\nduplicate.loc[1, \"ticket_id\"] = duplicate.loc[0, \"ticket_id\"]\nprint(rejection(duplicate))\n\nunknown = good.copy()\nunknown.loc[0, \"team\"] = \"sales\"\nprint(rejection(unknown))\n\n# Output:\n# accepted\n# Negative duration\n# Ticket IDs must be nonblank and unique\n# Unknown team"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A CSV has correct columns but includes infinite resolution_hours. Should it pass?","solution":"No. Check finiteness as well as numeric conversion and nonnegativity.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Reject infinity explicitly with a finiteness check.","Keep validation before summary generation."]},"quiz":{"question":"A duration of -3 converts to a finite float. Should the duration validator accept it?","options":["No. It also violates the nonnegative-duration rule.","Yes. Finite numeric values are always valid measurements.","Yes, if another ticket offsets it in the total."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Numeric conversion and finiteness are necessary checks, but durations must also satisfy the domain rule of zero or more hours. A plausible aggregate cannot repair an invalid row."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"One resolution_hours value is changed to -3. What does validate do?","options":["Raises ValueError: Negative duration","Accepts it, because it is finite","Treats it as zero"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Being a finite number is not enough. The nonnegative rule is checked before the breach flag."}]},{"id":"functions-and-tests","title":"20. Separate transformation from file output","takeaway":"Test calculations as functions before testing the exported report.","stage":"advanced","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A pure summary function takes a validated DataFrame and returns a table without writing files. This lets a test supply four rows and compare exact expected counts. Loading, validation, calculation and output can then fail separately with useful messages. Keep filenames and chart settings outside the arithmetic.","Test small cases whose results you can calculate by hand. Include the 24-hour boundary, unequal group sizes and a conflicting lookup. Avoid a test that calls the same aggregation expression to construct its expected value, because the implementation and test could repeat the same mistake. Floating point means may need a tolerance."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["This uses the kit's `summarize` function. Billing durations 4 and 28 and technical durations 24 and 48 give both teams two tickets and one breach, with means of 16 and 36 hours. These literal expectations catch several common aggregation errors."],"example":"import pandas as pd\nfrom analysis import summarize\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"ticket_id\": [\"B1\", \"B2\", \"T1\", \"T2\"],\n                   \"team\": [\"billing\", \"billing\", \"technical\", \"technical\"],\n                   \"resolution_hours\": [4, 28, 24, 48]})\ndf[\"breached\"] = (df[\"resolution_hours\"] > 24).astype(int)\n\ntable = summarize(df)\nassert table[\"tickets\"].tolist() == [2, 2]\nassert table[\"breaches\"].tolist() == [1, 1]\nassert table[\"mean_hours\"].tolist() == [16.0, 36.0]\nprint(table.to_string(index=False))\n\n# Output:\n#      team  tickets  breaches  breach_rate  mean_hours\n#   billing        2         1          0.5        16.0\n# technical        2         1          0.5        36.0"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"How should a test check that four-row example?","solution":"Assert the literal counts, breach counts and means for both teams.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Assert billing mean 16 and technical mean 36 independently.","Assert both groups contain two tickets and one breach."]},"quiz":{"question":"A test fixture has one team with durations 4 and 28 hours. The breach rule is > 24. What should the test assert?","options":["Count 2, breach count 1, mean 16 hours.","Whatever values the summary function returns on a second call.","Only that the function returns a table."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The two rows give independent expected values: (4 + 28) / 2 = 16 and only 28 breaches. Repeating the function or checking the output type cannot establish correct arithmetic."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"One customer_messages value is changed to 1.5. What does validate do?","options":["Raises ValueError: Message counts must be nonnegative whole numbers","Rounds it to 2","Accepts it, because it is numeric"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Counts must be whole numbers. Testing the validation function alone makes the failure quick to check, without writing a report."}]},{"id":"reproducible-notebooks","title":"21. Keep notebooks reproducible","stage":"advanced","takeaway":"A notebook's kernel remembers every cell you ran, in the order you ran it; a result counts only if a fresh kernel reproduces it by running all cells from the top.","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["Jupyter notebooks are good for exploration: code, output, charts and notes sit together. The kernel, however, keeps every variable you created in any order. Re-running an earlier cell after a later one, deleting a cell whose variable still exists, or editing a cell without re-running its dependants all produce outputs that the saved notebook cannot reproduce.","Before sharing a result, restart the kernel and run all cells, or execute the notebook from the command line (for example `jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute`). Read inputs from files with fixed paths and seeds, pin library versions, keep reusable transformations in a tested module (lesson 20) that the notebook imports, and clear large or sensitive outputs before committing. Use the notebook to explain; let the report command (lesson 23) produce the final numbers."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["The three cells are run once in the order a person might run them while exploring, and once top to bottom as a fresh kernel would. Re-running the conversion cell converts minutes to minutes again, so the exploratory total is 60 times too large, and nothing on screen says so. A restart and run-all exposes it."],"example":"cells = [\n    \"durations = [2, 30, 50, 4]                       # hours, loaded from the file\",\n    \"durations = [d * 60 for d in durations]          # convert to minutes\",\n    \"total_minutes = sum(durations)\",\n]\n\ndef run(order):\n    kernel = {}                                       # the variables a kernel keeps\n    for index in order:\n        exec(cells[index], kernel)\n    return kernel[\"total_minutes\"]\n\nprint(\"exploration order 0,1,2,1,2:\", run([0, 1, 2, 1, 2]))\nprint(\"fresh kernel order 0,1,2:\", run([0, 1, 2]))\n\n# Output:\n# exploration order 0,1,2,1,2: 309600\n# fresh kernel order 0,1,2: 5160"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"A colleague shares a notebook whose final chart shows 412 late tickets, but when you run it you get an error in cell 6 and, after fixing it, 388. Describe how you find out which result is right and what you change so this cannot recur.","solution":"Trust neither number until a fresh kernel reproduces one. Check the execution counts in the saved notebook for out-of-order runs, then restart and run all from the original input file and pinned environment, fixing cell 6 in the open. Compare intermediate row counts with the colleague's outputs to find where they diverge, often a variable defined by a deleted cell or a filter applied twice. Then move the transformations into a tested module, run the notebook headless in CI with nbconvert --execute, fix seeds and paths, pin versions, and have the report command produce the published count.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Reproduces the result from a fresh kernel before choosing one.","Uses execution counts and intermediate counts to locate the divergence.","Moves logic to a tested module and executes the notebook automatically."]},"quiz":{"question":"Why can a saved notebook show an output that running it again does not reproduce?","options":["Notebooks store outputs in a compressed format","The kernel kept state from cells run out of order, edited or deleted after they ran","Jupyter randomises cell order"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Outputs record what the kernel held at the time, not what the cells produce from the top."},"references":[{"title":"nbconvert: Executing notebooks","url":"https://nbconvert.readthedocs.io/en/latest/execute_api.html","section":"Executing notebooks from the command line","reviewed":"2026-11-08","scope":"nbconvert 7 documentation; the course practice uses a report command, and Jupyter is optional."},{"title":"Rule et al. (2019): Ten simple rules for writing and sharing computational analyses in Jupyter Notebooks","url":"https://journals.plos.org/ploscompbiol/article?id=10.1371/journal.pcbi.1007007","section":"Rules 4-9","reviewed":"2026-11-08","scope":"Peer-reviewed guidance used for workflow practices, not a tool requirement."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"In the lesson's simulation, what does the exploration order 0, 1, 2, 1, 2 report?","options":["5160","309600","86"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The conversion runs twice, multiplying by 3,600 instead of 60: 86 hours × 3,600 = 309,600."}]},{"id":"chunking-and-weighting","title":"22. Combine chunks using sufficient totals","stage":"advanced","takeaway":"Combine sums and counts rather than averaging unequal chunk means.","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["`read_csv` with `chunksize` can process a large file in smaller pieces. This limits the size of each loaded chunk, but a correct combined answer still needs state across chunks. A mean requires a total sum and a count of known values. Global duplicate detection also needs a shared set of IDs or an external store.","Do not assume chunking makes every operation easy. An exact median cannot generally be combined from chunk medians. Sorting across the whole dataset may need external sorting, and a lookup must still obey the same cardinality rule. Profile a real workload before adding chunking to this small 24-row example."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["A first chunk has two durations with mean 10 and a second has eight with mean 30. Keeping the sum and the count of known values gives the combined mean (2 × 10 + 8 × 30) / 10 = 26, not the unweighted mean 20."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\nchunks = [pd.Series([5, 15]), pd.Series([30] * 8)]\nprint(chunks[0].mean(), chunks[1].mean())\n\ntotal = sum(chunk.sum() for chunk in chunks)\nknown = sum(chunk.count() for chunk in chunks)\nprint(total / known)\nprint(sum(chunk.mean() for chunk in chunks) / len(chunks))\n\n# Output:\n# 10.0 30.0\n# 26.0\n# 20.0"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"How do you combine the two example chunks?","solution":"Carry sum 260 and count 10, then divide to get 26 hours.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Recover sums 20 and 240 from the two chunk means and counts.","Divide their combined sum by the combined count of ten."]},"quiz":{"question":"One chunk has 3 durations averaging 10 hours. Another has 1 duration averaging 30 hours. What is the combined mean?","options":["15 hours.","20 hours.","30 hours."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The first chunk contributes a sum of 30 and the second contributes 30. Divide their combined sum of 60 by four observations to get 15; do not average unequal-size chunk means equally."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/scale.html","section":"Use chunking","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; chunk means must be weighted by their observation counts."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"The CSV is read in chunks of 10 rows, and each chunk's sum and count are combined. What mean resolution time results?","options":["About 26.4 hours, the same as the whole file","The mean of the three chunk means","It cannot be computed from chunks"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Sums and counts combine exactly. Averaging chunk means would weight the final 4-row chunk as heavily as the 10-row ones."}]},{"id":"reproducible-report","title":"23. Generate a report from one command","takeaway":"Record inputs and versions so another run can explain its result.","stage":"advanced","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A reproducible report starts from named input files and runs without manual edits to intermediate tables. Save a source hash, row count and library versions beside the results. A hash identifies the exact bytes used; it does not certify data accuracy. Stable sort order and consistent numeric formatting make differences between runs easier to inspect.","The kit writes `report.json` and `counts.png` into a chosen output folder. The JSON includes the fictional-data limitation and the table behind the chart. Run it from a fresh folder and inspect the output before sharing. If dependency versions change, rerun the tests and compare the table, rather than assuming an image that looks similar proves equivalence."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["From the command line, `python analysis.py --out report-output` does the same as calling `run`. It produces both files, and running it twice with the same environment and input preserves the JSON content. A changed source would normally have a different SHA-256. Matching hashes are a practical identity check, not a mathematical guarantee that two files are identical."],"example":"import tempfile\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analysis import run\n\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as folder:\n    first, second = Path(folder, \"first\"), Path(folder, \"second\")\n    report = run(first)\n    run(second)\n    print(sorted(path.name for path in first.iterdir()))\n    first_json = (first / \"report.json\").read_text()\n    same = first_json == (second / \"report.json\").read_text()\n    print(report[\"rows\"], len(report[\"source_sha256\"]), same)\n\n# Output:\n# ['counts.png', 'report.json']\n# 24 64 True"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Two reports disagree. What should you compare first?","solution":"Compare the input hashes, filtering rules and dependency versions before interpreting the difference.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Compare the exact input hashes before assuming the data is unchanged.","Check analysis choices and actual package versions."]},"quiz":{"question":"Two reports have the same input hash and dependency versions, but different totals. What should you inspect next?","options":["The code revision, filters and analysis parameters used by each run.","Assume identical input guarantees identical calculations.","Keep whichever report has the newer file timestamp."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Identical inputs can produce different results when calculation rules change. Recorded code and parameters help explain the difference; file timestamps do not establish correctness."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."},{"title":"Python hashlib","url":"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/hashlib.html","section":"Hash objects and hexdigest","scope":"Hash API behaviour. Collision resistance is practical evidence, not proof that distinct inputs can never share a digest.","reviewed":"2026-11-07"}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"Which provenance does report.json record?","options":["The source file's SHA-256 and the Python, NumPy, pandas and Matplotlib versions","Only the run date","The username that ran it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The hash identifies the exact input bytes, and the versions identify the libraries, so a different result can be traced to code or parameters."}]},{"id":"sensitivity-and-limits","title":"24. Check how choices change the conclusion","takeaway":"Show when a result depends on a threshold or exclusion rule.","stage":"advanced","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A breach rate depends on the policy threshold. Recalculate at 12, 24 and 48 hours to see the effect without redefining the official 24-hour result. Sensitivity analysis asks how a conclusion changes under stated alternatives. It does not let you pick whichever threshold produces the preferred story.","Keep the same population when comparing thresholds so only one choice changes. If you also remove long tickets, you cannot attribute the difference to the threshold alone. Report missing-data assumptions separately. For this fictional file, the exercise tests calculation stability and honest description rather than an operational policy recommendation."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["Durations 8, 24, 40 and 60 breach thresholds of 12, 24 and 48 hours at rates of 75%, 50% and 25%. Equality remains nonbreaching in each case."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\nhours = pd.Series([8, 24, 40, 60])\nfor threshold in [12, 24, 48]:\n    print(threshold, (hours > threshold).mean())\n\n# Output:\n# 12 0.75\n# 24 0.5\n# 48 0.25"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"At a threshold of 24 hours, how many of [8, 24, 40, 60] breach?","solution":"Two of four breach, giving 50%; exactly 24 does not breach.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Count 40 and 60 as the only values above 24.","Keep the same four records while changing an alternate threshold."]},"quiz":{"question":"For durations [8, 24, 40, 60], change the strict breach threshold from > 24 to > 40. What happens?","options":["The rate falls from 50% to 25% on the same four tickets.","The rate stays at 50% because 40 still counts.","The rate rises because the threshold is larger."],"correct":0,"explanation":"At > 24, both 40 and 60 breach. At > 40, only 60 breaches. Keeping the same records isolates the effect of the changed threshold."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"What does threshold_rates report for strict thresholds of 12, 24 and 48 hours?","options":["About 66.7%, 41.7% and 12.5%","About 41.7% at every threshold","About 12.5%, 41.7% and 66.7%"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The same 24 tickets give very different rates as the threshold moves, which is why a conclusion should state its threshold."}]},{"id":"handoff-to-modeling","title":"25. Hand analysis to a model without leaking the answer","takeaway":"Separate intake information from outcomes before attempting prediction.","stage":"advanced","sections":[{"title":"Understand the decision","paragraphs":["A descriptive analysis can use `resolution_hours` and `breached` because it summarizes completed tickets. A model intended to act at intake cannot use either as a predictor. The duration is only known later and the flag is the target. Mark when every field becomes available, not only whether its type is numeric.","Keep ticket IDs for tracing errors, but arbitrary identifiers rarely explain the underlying process. A model also needs a split that respects time or repeated customers, an untouched evaluation set and a baseline. The next learning path uses a larger fictional dataset to practise those decisions. Good results on that synthetic exercise do not establish production usefulness."]},{"title":"Worked example","paragraphs":["In this kit, `customer_messages` means messages supplied at intake, so it can be a feature. If another export counted messages over the entire case, the same column name would leak future information. The data dictionary determines which interpretation is valid."],"example":"import pandas as pd\n\nknown_at_intake = {\"created_date\", \"channel\", \"priority\", \"team\",\n                   \"customer_messages\"}\ncolumns = pd.read_csv(\"support_tickets.csv\").columns\n\nprint([c for c in columns if c in known_at_intake])\nprint([c for c in columns if c not in known_at_intake and c != \"ticket_id\"])\n\n# Output:\n# ['created_date', 'channel', 'priority', 'team', 'customer_messages']\n# ['resolution_hours', 'breached']"}],"exercise":{"prompt":"Which of resolution_hours and channel is available for intake prediction?","solution":"Channel is available at intake; resolution_hours is an outcome and must be excluded.","solutionFormat":"prose","checks":["Identify when each candidate feature becomes available.","Exclude duration and the target itself from intake predictors."]},"quiz":{"question":"A model predicts delay when a ticket arrives. Which field can be a predictor if it is recorded at that moment?","options":["The priority assigned at intake.","The final resolution timestamp.","A customer rating submitted after closure."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Intake priority is available at the decision time. Resolution timestamps and later ratings use future information, even if they correlate strongly with delay."},"references":[{"title":"pandas user guide","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","section":"Selection, missing data, operations and merge","reviewed":"2026-10-02","scope":"pandas 3.0.6 API; examples use fictional ticket data."}],"extraQuiz":[{"question":"A model predicts delay when a ticket arrives. Which two dataset columns must it not use as predictors?","options":["resolution_hours and breached","channel and priority","team and created_date"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Both are outcomes known only after the ticket is resolved. Using them would leak the answer into the model."}]}],"stages":[{"id":"foundation","title":"Inspect the support export","description":"Load the supplied file, document one-row-per-ticket grain, check types and produce a short quality summary.","exitCriteria":["Check 24 rows and 24 unique IDs.","Calculate known duration count without replacing missing values with zero.","Demonstrate how one conflicting duplicate changes validation."],"project":{"title":"Inspect the support export","brief":"Load the supplied file, document one-row-per-ticket grain, check types and produce a short quality summary.","requirements":["Check 24 rows and 24 unique IDs.","Calculate known duration count without replacing missing values with zero.","Demonstrate how one conflicting duplicate changes validation."],"rubric":["Check 24 rows and 24 unique IDs.","Calculate known duration count without replacing missing values with zero.","Demonstrate how one conflicting duplicate changes validation."],"solution":"The supplied clean file has 24 unique tickets and no missing required values. Keep its raw bytes unchanged. Reject the conflicting duplicate; do not choose a duration silently.","solutionFormat":"prose"},"stageCheck":[{"question":"The breached column is dropped before validate runs. What happens?","options":["Raises ValueError: Missing required columns","The flag is recomputed from resolution_hours","The report is written without breach rates"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The required columns are checked first, so a partial export fails before any calculation."},{"question":"One channel value is changed to phone. What does validate do?","options":["Raises ValueError: Unknown channel","Accepts it as a new channel","Treats it as email"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each category column has an allowed set, so a new value needs a deliberate decision rather than a silent new group."},{"question":"validate receives the dataset's columns with no rows. What happens?","options":["Raises ValueError: Required values must be present in a nonempty table","Returns an empty table","Reports a breach rate of 0"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An empty export would otherwise produce rates with no denominator, so it is rejected."}]},{"id":"intermediate","title":"Compare teams without multiplying rows","description":"Attach team owners and calculate ticket counts, breach counts, rates and mean durations. Produce a labeled count chart.","exitCriteria":["Enforce a many-to-one lookup and reject unmatched teams.","Calculate the overall rate from ticket counts.","Show a histogram separately from the category comparison."],"project":{"title":"Compare teams without multiplying rows","brief":"Attach team owners and calculate ticket counts, breach counts, rates and mean durations. Produce a labeled count chart.","requirements":["Enforce a many-to-one lookup and reject unmatched teams.","Calculate the overall rate from ticket counts.","Show a histogram separately from the category comparison."],"rubric":["Enforce a many-to-one lookup and reject unmatched teams.","Calculate the overall rate from ticket counts.","Show a histogram separately from the category comparison."],"solution":"Use validate=\"many_to_one\" plus merge indicator checks. Each team has 12 tickets. Keep the all-ticket denominator at 24. Report the actual calculated breach counts; the chart labels ticket counts, not hours.","solutionFormat":"prose"},"stageCheck":[{"question":"teams.csv loses its technical row. What does attach_owners do?","options":["Raises ValueError: Unmatched team","Leaves the technical owners blank","Drops the technical tickets"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The merge indicator shows rows that found no owner, and the function refuses to continue rather than lose them."},{"question":"How many rows does attach_owners return for the supplied files?","options":["24, the same as the input","48","2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each ticket matches exactly one team row, so the join adds an owner column without changing the grain."},{"question":"What mean resolution hours does summarize report for billing and technical?","options":["About 18.2 for billing and 34.7 for technical","About 26.4 for both","About 34.7 for billing and 18.2 for technical"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Technical has the four longest tickets, so its mean is much higher even though both teams have 12 tickets."}]},{"id":"advanced","title":"Deliver a reproducible analysis","description":"Create one command that validates the input and writes a report, chart and input provenance. Explain the limits of the fictional sample.","exitCriteria":["Test negative, infinite and conflicting values.","Record source hash and library versions.","Compare strict thresholds of 12, 24 and 48 hours without changing rows.","Identify fields unavailable at prediction time."],"project":{"title":"Deliver a reproducible analysis","brief":"Create one command that validates the input and writes a report, chart and input provenance. Explain the limits of the fictional sample.","requirements":["Test negative, infinite and conflicting values.","Record source hash and library versions.","Compare strict thresholds of 12, 24 and 48 hours without changing rows.","Identify fields unavailable at prediction time."],"rubric":["Test negative, infinite and conflicting values.","Record source hash and library versions.","Compare strict thresholds of 12, 24 and 48 hours without changing rows.","Identify fields unavailable at prediction time."],"solution":"Run the independent tests before generating output. Include the 24-row count, table, source hash and limitation in report.json. Treat alternate thresholds as sensitivity checks; exclude outcome fields from intake prediction.","solutionFormat":"prose"},"stageCheck":[{"question":"One resolution_hours value is changed to inf in the CSV. What does validate do?","options":["Raises ValueError: Nonfinite resolution_hours","Accepts it as a very long ticket","Treats it as missing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"pandas reads inf as a float, so the finiteness check, not type conversion, is what rejects it."},{"question":"run() is called twice on the same files, writing to two folders. Are the two report.json files identical?","options":["Yes, byte for byte","No: each contains its run time","No: the keys are in a different order"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The report holds only values computed from the input and library versions, and it is written with sorted keys."},{"question":"A blank line is appended to a copy of the CSV, and run() reads that copy. What changes in report.json?","options":["Only source_sha256: it still reports 24 rows","Nothing at all","rows becomes 25"],"correct":0,"explanation":"pandas skips blank lines, but the hash covers every byte, so the provenance shows the input file changed."}]}],"downloads":[{"title":"README.md","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/README.md"},{"title":"support_tickets.csv","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/support_tickets.csv"},{"title":"teams.csv","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/teams.csv"},{"title":"analysis.py","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/analysis.py"},{"title":"test_analysis.py","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/test_analysis.py"},{"title":"test_dataset.py","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/test_dataset.py"},{"title":"requirements.txt","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/requirements.txt"},{"title":"projects.md","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/projects.md"}],"resources":{"folder":"data-analysis-python-practice","files":[{"id":"README-md","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/README.md","role":"guide","description":"README.md"},{"id":"support_tickets-csv","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/support_tickets.csv","role":"data","description":"support tickets.csv"},{"id":"teams-csv","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/teams.csv","role":"data","description":"teams.csv"},{"id":"analysis-py","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/analysis.py","role":"reference","description":"analysis.py"},{"id":"test_analysis-py","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/test_analysis.py","role":"test","description":"test analysis.py"},{"id":"test_dataset-py","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/test_dataset.py","role":"test","description":"test dataset.py"},{"id":"requirements-txt","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/requirements.txt","role":"guide","description":"requirements.txt"},{"id":"projects-md","href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice/projects.md","role":"guide","description":"projects.md"}],"tasks":[{"id":"foundation","title":"Inspect the support export","goal":"Load the supplied file, document one-row-per-ticket grain, check types and produce a short quality summary.","fileIds":["README-md","support_tickets-csv","teams-csv","analysis-py","test_analysis-py","test_dataset-py","requirements-txt","projects-md"],"steps":["Attempt the stage brief in projects.md before opening the reference.","Run the dataset checks; install optional analysis dependencies for the full lab.","Compare your result with the reference and explain the stated limits."],"commands":[{"label":"Dataset checks, standard library only","command":"python -m unittest -v test_dataset.py","expected":"All dataset tests pass."},{"label":"Analysis checks after installing requirements","command":"python -m unittest -v test_analysis.py","expected":"All analysis tests pass."},{"label":"Generate the report","command":"python analysis.py --out report-output","expected":"report-output contains report.json and counts.png."}],"prerequisites":[],"notes":["Dataset checks use Python 3.14+. Executable analysis uses Python 3.14+ and the pinned NumPy, pandas and Matplotlib dependencies. Tested with Python 3.14.","The input is fictional and cannot support real service or causal claims."]},{"id":"intermediate","title":"Compare teams without multiplying rows","goal":"Attach team owners and calculate ticket counts, breach counts, rates and mean durations. Produce a labeled count chart.","fileIds":["README-md","support_tickets-csv","teams-csv","analysis-py","test_analysis-py","test_dataset-py","requirements-txt","projects-md"],"steps":["Attempt the stage brief in projects.md before opening the reference.","Run the dataset checks; install optional analysis dependencies for the full lab.","Compare your result with the reference and explain the stated limits."],"commands":[{"label":"Dataset checks, standard library only","command":"python -m unittest -v test_dataset.py","expected":"All dataset tests pass."},{"label":"Analysis checks after installing requirements","command":"python -m unittest -v test_analysis.py","expected":"All analysis tests pass."},{"label":"Generate the report","command":"python analysis.py --out report-output","expected":"report-output contains report.json and counts.png."}],"prerequisites":[],"notes":["Dataset checks use Python 3.14+. Executable analysis uses Python 3.14+ and the pinned NumPy, pandas and Matplotlib dependencies. Tested with Python 3.14.","The input is fictional and cannot support real service or causal claims."]},{"id":"advanced","title":"Deliver a reproducible analysis","goal":"Create one command that validates the input and writes a report, chart and input provenance. Explain the limits of the fictional sample.","fileIds":["README-md","support_tickets-csv","teams-csv","analysis-py","test_analysis-py","test_dataset-py","requirements-txt","projects-md"],"steps":["Attempt the stage brief in projects.md before opening the reference.","Run the dataset checks; install optional analysis dependencies for the full lab.","Compare your result with the reference and explain the stated limits."],"commands":[{"label":"Dataset checks, standard library only","command":"python -m unittest -v test_dataset.py","expected":"All dataset tests pass."},{"label":"Analysis checks after installing requirements","command":"python -m unittest -v test_analysis.py","expected":"All analysis tests pass."},{"label":"Generate the report","command":"python analysis.py --out report-output","expected":"report-output contains report.json and counts.png."}],"prerequisites":[],"notes":["Dataset checks use Python 3.14+. Executable analysis uses Python 3.14+ and the pinned NumPy, pandas and Matplotlib dependencies. Tested with Python 3.14.","The input is fictional and cannot support real service or causal claims."]}],"lessonTasks":{"question-and-grain":"foundation","load-and-inspect":"foundation","numpy-arrays":"foundation","types-and-dates":"foundation","select-and-filter":"foundation","missing-and-duplicates":"foundation","text-cleaning":"foundation","indexes-and-alignment":"foundation","derive-and-validate":"intermediate","vectorize-not-apply":"intermediate","group-and-denominators":"intermediate","joins-and-cardinality":"intermediate","distributions-and-outliers":"intermediate","correlation-and-outliers":"intermediate","dates-and-aggregation":"intermediate","charts-for-questions":"intermediate","reshape-for-comparison":"intermediate","association-and-comparison":"advanced","validation-functions":"advanced","functions-and-tests":"advanced","reproducible-notebooks":"advanced","reproducible-report":"advanced","sensitivity-and-limits":"advanced","handoff-to-modeling":"advanced","chunking-and-weighting":"advanced"},"bundle":{"href":"paths/data-analysis-python/practice-bundle.zip"}}});
